0.18,1.18,0.17,0.7,-0.18,0.73,-1.33,-0.38,0.86,-1.61,-0.91,0.21,-0.22,0.35,2.03,-0.96,-0.82,0.62,0.15,-1.37,-1.08,-1.1,1.3,-0.76],"paragraph":"Mar–May in Jakarta has warmed by about 0.2 °C per decade since 1951, roughly 1.5 °C in all, and the hottest days have warmed faster than the coldest (about 0.2 vs 0.15 °C per decade). There is a strong, well-established El Niño effect on temperature: after El Niño conditions in the preceding Sep–Nov, Mar–May tends to run warmer, and El Niño accounts for about 30% of the year-to-year ups and downs. With this year's strong El Niño, that push is about 1.5 times a normal year-to-year swing, roughly as much warmth as 20 years of the long-term trend. Without El Niño, a Mar–May this warm would come about once every 20 years. Mar–May rainfall shows no clear long-term trend, but El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a wetter season with more rainy days and more intense heavy-rain days, and this year's shift is substantial. Relative humidity has fallen by about 0.4 points per decade, roughly 3 points since 1951; because warmer air holds more moisture, this does not necessarily mean the air is drier. El Niño has a strong, well-established effect toward a more humid Mar–May, and this year's shift is larger than a normal year-to-year swing. For Mar–May 2027, a warmer-than-normal season is very likely (more than 95% chance), about 0.8 °C above the 1991–2020 average, mostly because of the long-term warming trend rather than El Niño; a wetter-than-normal season is likely (about 75% chance). The El Niño expected for Sep–Nov 2026 is stronger than any in the record used here, so the outlook extends the historical relationship beyond what has been observed. This outlook is based on the El Niño strength expected for Sep–Nov 2026 and will be updated when it is observed in early December.","variables":{"temperature":{"normal_1991_2020":26.964,"trend_per_decade":0.1948,"trend_p":0.0,"trend_total_since_1951":1.4608,"enso_effect_per_deg